WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.113 --> 00:00:02.480
(upbeat classical music)

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.260
- [Dennis] If you plucked people
from all across the country

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.760
and said we have a farm,
the image that they have

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.053
of who's that farmer would
not be Kristyn Leach.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:19.960
- My name is Kristyn Leach,

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.610
and I'm a farmer in Winters, California;

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.430
and I grow predominantly
Korean and East Asian herbs

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:26.583
and vegetables.

00:00:31.521 --> 00:00:32.540
(gentle music)

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.160
I was born in Daegu, Korea;
I was adopted as an infant;

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.160
I grew up on Long Island in New York.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.310
The regional cuisine of Long
Island is pizza and bagels,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.510
so that's what I was
just sort of raised on.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.830
I just had never really
explored Korean heritage,

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:54.970
but I did get curious about the food.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.040
Oh wait, this is a really good little one.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.530
I worked on organic farms
when I was a teenager,

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.930
and so I just thought like,
oh, well, I know how to grow

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.590
plants, maybe I should
grow some of these plants

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:10.270
from Korea just to better
understand this place

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:11.103
where I'm from.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.460
I went back to Korea for
the first time since I left.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.410
I spent just like three
weeks traveling around

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.070
and was learning from all of these farmers

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.823
and visiting different farms.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:30.480
It felt like a pretty profound way

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.920
to actually engage with identity

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.670
because it answered for me a sense of

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.590
what place I could find within, like,

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.173
a bigger story of Korean history.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.243
(upbeat music)

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.290
I practice a style of
farming that's referred to

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.220
as natural farming in Korea and Japan.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.880
Everything on the farm
is grown organically,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.600
and, essentially, it's
the way just like peasant

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.253
subsistence farmers farm.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.670
Some of the heritage crops
that we focus on here are

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.650
the Korean sesame leaf,

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:10.500
the flavor is kind of licore-y and minty.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.290
Chamoe, Korean melon, is
somewhere between like a cucumber

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.720
and melon in flavor; it's
really, really crunchy.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.180
Chili peppers, a really beloved staple,

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.950
and Japanese eggplant, just
a small, baseball-sized

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.633
eggplant; it's super
dense and really creamy.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.220
Three times a week, I'll
usually head to the Bay

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.726
to make a delivery.

00:02:36.726 --> 00:02:38.720
(upbeat music)

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.730
Namu Gaji is a restaurant
in the Mission District

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.420
in San Francisco, all of
the produce goes directly

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:45.630
to their restaurant.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:46.543
- Hey, Kristyn!

00:02:46.543 --> 00:02:47.937
- Hey, how's it going?

00:02:47.937 --> 00:02:50.370
And their chefs and cooks figure out

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.323
how to kind of design a menu around it.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.190
- My name's Dennis Lee.
I'm the chef and owner

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.257
of Namu Gaji in San Francisco.

00:03:01.251 --> 00:03:02.084
(upbeat classical music)

00:03:02.084 --> 00:03:05.270
Right now we're doing
a really nice, simple

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:09.430
grilled eggplant dish with
a really heavy dusting

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.370
of minced perilla.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.300
We're doing a nice melon salad right now

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.560
with watermelon and Korean melon.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.770
We have a chili oil that
we serve with our ramen

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.053
that has Korean chilis from the farm.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.610
It's a restaurant that's
operated by Korean Americans,

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:26.983
so you see that in what we do.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.570
The way that our relationship
works with Kristyn

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:36.130
is totally unique because
it's tied to who she is.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.990
To be able to support an Asian woman

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:42.460
and co-create a platform
to express ourselves

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.903
and share our story with
people, that's pretty special.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:48.870
- The food that Dennis is making

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.820
allows itself to just be really original,

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.430
and it doesn't feel like
it's trying to just reproduce

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:59.223
a mold of what is Korean
or what is Asian-American.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.290
I'm learning about, like, my lineage;

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:05.880
I'm trying to figure out
a sense of belonging.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.000
The experience of adoption
is so complicated.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.930
I feel really grateful to have farming

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.080
be the way that I
interact with my culture.

